# V. Risk factors

# 5.1 AI capital cycle risk
The current Afino boom has benefited mainly from the supercycle of AI data centre construction. IT data communications account for 38% of revenue, up to 110%. If AI’s capital spending normalizes or slows in the next 2-3 years (the cyclical nature of its history of capital investment in science and technology) the growth rate of Afino will fall sharply, potentially triggering a double-dumping of valuation and profitability.

#5.2 ComScope CCS merger risk
CommScope CCS mergers and acquisitions of $10.5 billion are the largest deal in the history of Anfino, accounting for about 46% of the previous market value. Total liabilities have soared to about $18.7 billion.

#5.3 China Tax Dispute
And, more importantly, this could set a precedent for similar disputes in China in the future. China is an important manufacturing base for Anfino, and geopolitical tensions increase uncertainty about how to operate in China.

# 5.4 Increased competition
The high profits in AI’s interconnection are attracting more input from Molex, TE Connectivity, and low-cost regional manufacturers. Rapid developments in light interconnection technologies can also change competition patterns.

#5.5 Leveraging up
The net debt may reach about $20 billion as a result of ComScope’s merger. In a high interest rate environment, debt service pressures increase, which may limit future M&A capacity and shareholder returns.

# 5.6 Internals are down
The executives of CEO Richard Adam Norwitt and others have recently lost a large share. While the executives have lost ownership for a number of reasons (tax planning, diversification, etc.), the decline in ownership during the stock exchange is a matter of concern.

